course: biology
lesson: 352
title: Teeth: enamel, sugar, holes, brushing and the dentist
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The outside of every tooth is enamel, the hardest stuff in your whole body, harder even than bone, but it has one enemy: acid.
explain: Acid does not come from sweets directly. It comes from bacteria living in a soft sticky film on your teeth, called plaque, which feast on sugar and spit out acid.
explain: Think of an afternoon of nibbling sweets: every nibble starts a new acid attack that can last up to about an hour, so ten small snacks hurt teeth far more than one pudding after dinner.
explain: Brushing with fluoride toothpaste scrubs the plaque away and makes the enamel tougher, and a dentist can spot and fill a small hole long before it hurts.
fact: Enamel, the outer layer of a tooth, is the hardest material in the human body, harder than bone, but acid can soften it, and lost enamel cannot grow back.
fact: Plaque is a soft film of bacteria that builds up on teeth every day; the bacteria eat sugar and make acid that eats into enamel.
fact: How often you eat sugar matters more than how much, because each sugary snack starts an acid attack that can last up to about an hour.
fact: Disclosing tablets colour plaque pink so you can see where you missed, and plaque left for days can harden into tartar, which only a dentist or hygienist can scrape off.
fact: Brush twice a day for about two minutes with fluoride toothpaste, then spit but do not rinse, so the fluoride stays on the teeth; fluoride makes enamel stronger against acid.
fact: Some places add a small amount of fluoride to tap water to protect teeth.
fact: A hole made by acid is called a cavity or decay; the dentist cleans it out and fills it, and small holes do not hurt, which is why checkups matter.
fact: Under the enamel is dentine, full of tiny tubes leading towards the pulp, the soft middle with the nerve; if enamel or gum wears thin, cold or heat reaches the nerve and the tooth zings.
fact: Even sugar-free fizzy drinks are acidic and can soften enamel; water and milk are the safest drinks for teeth.
fact: Cheese and sugar-free gum after a meal make you produce more saliva, which washes teeth and helps bring the mouth's acid back to normal.
fact: Long ago people chewed twigs into brushes, like the miswak stick still used today; the Chinese made bristle brushes hundreds of years ago, William Addis made toothbrushes in England around 1780, and nylon bristles came in 1938.
fact: George Washington's false teeth were made of hippo ivory, metal and human and animal teeth, not wood, which would soften and rot in a wet mouth.
exercise: What is the hardest material in the human body? | Tooth enamel | The thigh bone | Your fingernails | The skull bone
exercise: What makes the acid that damages teeth? | Bacteria in plaque eating sugar | Sugar melting away in the saliva | The tongue rubbing against the teeth | Cold air breathed through the mouth
exercise: What is plaque? | A soft sticky film of bacteria on the teeth | A hard white layer that grows over the teeth to protect them | A bit of leftover toothpaste on the gums | A crack in the enamel
exercise: Which is worse for teeth? | Ten small sugary snacks through the day | One big sweet pudding eaten straight after dinner | Both are exactly the same | Neither, sugar never harms teeth
exercise: What does a disclosing tablet do? | Colours plaque so you can see it | Whitens your teeth for good, like bleach | Fills in any tiny holes in the teeth | Kills all the bacteria in the mouth
exercise: What is tartar? | Plaque that has hardened and needs a dentist to remove | A kind of chewy sweet that sticks to your teeth for hours | The soft middle of a tooth | A tooth that has not come through yet
exercise: How long and how often should you brush your teeth? | About two minutes, twice a day | Ten seconds after every single meal | Five minutes, but only once a week | Only when the teeth feel dirty or sticky
exercise: What should you do after brushing with fluoride toothpaste? | Spit, but do not rinse with water | Rinse well three times with clean water | Swallow all the toothpaste foam down | Eat a sweet to take the taste away
exercise: What does fluoride do for teeth? | Makes enamel stronger against acid | Makes the teeth grow longer and faster | Dissolves the old, worn enamel away | Feeds the good bacteria living in plaque
exercise: Why should you see the dentist even when nothing hurts? | Small holes do not hurt yet, and are easy to fill | Teeth only start to decay when you stop going for checkups | The dentist has to count your teeth each year | Painless teeth are always rotten inside
exercise: Why does cold ice cream make a tooth zing? | Cold reaches the nerve through tiny tubes in worn dentine | The ice freezes the whole tooth solid for a second or two | Ice cream has acid that burns the nerve | The tongue gets too cold and hurts
exercise: Can a sugar-free fizzy drink harm teeth? | Yes, it is acidic and can soften enamel | No, only sugar can harm teeth | No, the bubbles clean the teeth | Yes, but only if it is warm
exercise: Which drinks are safest for teeth? | Water and milk | Cola and lemonade | Fruit juice and squash | Energy drinks and sports drinks
exercise: Why can cheese after a meal help teeth? | It makes more saliva, which washes and protects teeth | It glues a brand new layer of enamel onto every tooth | It kills every germ in the mouth | It fills small holes with calcium
exercise: What did people use before plastic toothbrushes? | Chewed twigs, like the miswak stick | Nothing at all, ever, in the whole of history | Their fingernails only | Metal spoons
exercise: What were George Washington's false teeth made of? | Hippo ivory, metal and real teeth | Wood carved from the cherry trees in his garden | Glass and silver | Painted stones
test: «had sweets all arvo but only little ones, that's fine for me teeth innit» What is right? | No, each nibble starts a new acid attack, so all day is the worst way | Yes, small sweets are too little to harm teeth, however many you have | Yes, as long as they are not chocolate | No, but only because sweets are hard
test: «it says sugar free on the can, so I can sip it all day, no harm» What is the truth? | It is still acidic, so sipping all day keeps softening enamel | Sugar-free drinks clean the teeth | It is fine if you brush after the last sip | Only the bubbles harm teeth, not the drink
test: A boy chews a disclosing tablet and his back teeth go bright pink. What does that tell him? | He has been missing the back teeth when brushing | His back teeth are rotten right through and must come out | The tablet only works on back teeth | His back teeth are stronger than the front
test: «brushed, then rinsed loads with water so it's proper clean» What did he get wrong? | Rinsing washes away the fluoride that should stay on the teeth | Nothing, rinsing lots with water is the most important part of brushing | He should have used hot water | He should rinse before brushing instead
test: What would happen if nobody cleaned plaque off a tooth for weeks? | It could harden into tartar that only a dentist can remove | It would turn into new enamel | It would fall off by itself each night | It would make the tooth harder
test: «tooth doesn't hurt so there's no hole, don't need the dentist» What is right? | Holes start small and painless, so checkups catch them early | No pain always means there is no hole, so there is no need to go | Holes always hurt from the first day | Dentists only look at teeth that hurt
test: «my nan says Washington had wooden teeth, everyone knows that» What is the truth? | His false teeth were ivory, metal and real teeth, not wood | Yes, oak teeth carved by a carpenter | Yes, and they lasted his whole life | No, he had all his own teeth
test: In a cartoon a character bites a metal can and his teeth shatter like glass, then he grins with a gap that grows back. What is right? | Enamel is very hard but can chip, and adult teeth do not grow back | Teeth are as weak as glass, and a lost grown-up tooth grows back in a day or two | A gap in a grown-up's teeth refills in a day | Teeth are softer than tin cans
test: «song's 2 minutes, I brush through the whole song» Is that about right? | Yes, about two minutes is right | No, one second is enough | No, you need a whole hour | No, a song is far too long
test: Both a hot drink and an icy drink make the same tooth zing. What is the most likely reason? | Worn enamel or gum lets heat and cold reach the nerve | The tooth only feels hot things | The drinks contain acid that burns | The tongue is sore, not the tooth
test: On a desert island with no toothbrush, what would help clean your teeth best? | Chewing the end of a twig into a brush | Rubbing them hard with handfuls of wet sand | Eating dried fruit all day | Drinking seawater
exam: Identify the principal origin of the acid responsible for the decay of dental enamel. | Bacteria within dental plaque, which metabolise sugar | Sugar, which dissolves spontaneously into acid | Saliva, which is acidic in all circumstances | Cold air inhaled through the mouth
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the significance of the frequency of sugar consumption? | Frequency is of greater consequence than quantity, each intake initiating a fresh acid attack | Quantity alone is of consequence, the frequency of consumption being wholly immaterial to the teeth | Sugar consumed in small portions is wholly harmless | Sugar consumed at mealtimes causes the greatest harm
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning beverages free of sugar. | Such beverages, being acidic, may nonetheless soften dental enamel | Such beverages exert a cleansing effect upon the teeth | Such beverages are without any effect upon the teeth | Such beverages strengthen enamel by virtue of their carbonation
exam: State the recommended practice subsequent to brushing with a fluoride toothpaste. | The residue is to be spat out without rinsing | The mouth is to be rinsed thoroughly with water | The toothpaste is to be swallowed | A sweet is to be consumed forthwith
